Subject: Compare a Number and a BigInt without losing the sign

What this does
  Corrects the mixed Number/BigInt comparison so the result does not depend on
  a sign that was discarded on the way in.

Why we need it
  The mixed path reduced both operands to a common form before ordering them,
  which loses which operand is negative and can report the order inverted.

What it adds
  The comparison takes account of which operand is negative and which
  representation it holds before deciding the order. Values that are equal compare
  equal, NaN keeps its unordered result, and same-type comparisons are untouched.
  No opcode is added or retired and BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -15189,10 +15189,16 @@
         /* compute f so that 2^f <= abs(a) < 2^(f+1) */
         a_mant = js_bigint_get_mant_exp(ctx, &f, a);
         if (f != e) {
-            if (f < e)
-                return -1;
+            /* a and b have the same sign here (the a_sign != b_sign case was
+               handled above), so the operand of larger magnitude is the larger
+               number when both are positive and the SMALLER when both are
+               negative.  Ordering by exponent alone answers the negative case
+               backwards.  The two expressions below are the same sign
+               convention the f == e arm and js_bigint_cmp already use. */
+            if (f < e)
+                return 2 * a_sign - 1;   /* abs(a) < abs(b) */
             else
-                return 1;
+                return 1 - 2 * a_sign;   /* abs(a) > abs(b) */
         } else {
             mant = (mant | ((uint64_t)1 << 52)) << 11; /* align to a_mant */
